          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry you're having trouble sleeping. The safety check found no emergency signs in what you described. That does not mean nothing is wrong, so it's worth talking to a clinician if the problem continues.\n\nThe General clinical reference on insomnia (condition-insomnia) describes insomnia as difficulty falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, despite having enough time to sleep, together with problems during the day. It says that sleeping a certain number of hours is not the test. What matters is whether poor sleep affects how you function. The same reference describes chronic insomnia disorder as trouble sleeping at least three nights a week for at least three months. Your timeframe matches that description, but only a clinician can tell whether it applies to you.\n\nThe passage I was given does not cover causes, tests, or treatments, so I can't say what might be behind your sleep problem or what helps. A clinician can ask about your sleep pattern, your daytime symptoms, and your health history, and can discuss options with you.\n\nSeek medical advice soon if your sleep problems are getting worse, or if you have new symptoms you can't explain.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
